WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.719
OT business continuity protects critical physical operations, not just servers.

00:00:05.719 --> 00:00:18.898
It defines how the organization maintains an acceptable service, shifts to a safe degraded mode, performs a controlled shutdown, and restores trustworthy operation after disruption.

00:00:18.898 --> 00:00:30.088
Planning begins with essential processes and their dependencies: controllers, HMIs, engineering systems, networks, utilities, people, vendors, spares, facilities, and downstream operations.

00:00:30.088 --> 00:00:39.786
Recovery objectives must reflect process state, safety, data tolerance, and the real time needed for validation and restart.

00:00:39.786 --> 00:00:46.997
Backups should cover logic, configurations, system images, data, software, licenses, and vendor-specific requirements.

00:00:46.997 --> 00:00:51.224
Protected copies and restore testing prove recoverability.

00:00:51.224 --> 00:00:59.927
Manual fallback is useful only when equipment, procedures, staffing, and training make it safe and sustainable.

00:00:59.927 --> 00:01:09.128
During cyber incidents, continuity and incident response coordinate containment, fallback, safe shutdown, evidence preservation, and deliberate recovery.

00:01:09.128 --> 00:01:15.841
Speed alone cannot justify reconnecting systems whose integrity or dependencies remain uncertain.

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:25.539
Strong programs exercise decisions, drill procedures, test restoration, maintain contacts and diagrams, assign owners, and close lessons learned.

00:01:25.539 --> 00:01:32.253
Continuity is a practiced organizational capability aligned with safety and operational reality.

00:01:32.253 --> 00:01:40.956
Its purpose is safer, more predictable recovery of the mission when normal systems and assumptions fail.
